  "prediction": "**Summary of Recent News**\n\n**Internet Archive and Wayback Machine**\n\nAlexa Internet has been donating its crawl data to the Internet Archive since 1996. The data is added to the Wayback Machine after an embargo period, providing a valuable resource for researchers and historians.\n\n**London School of Economics (LSE) Incident**\n\nThe LSE is investigating allegations of a Nazi-themed drinking game that led to a brawl involving a Jewish student. The incident occurred during a skiing trip to Val d'Isère, organized by the students' union, which was attended by 150 students from the university's athletics union. According to reports, the game involved playing cards arranged in the shape of a swastika, with players required to \"salute the Führer.\" A Jewish student objected to the game, leading to a physical altercation that resulted in a broken nose.\n\nThe LSE has condemned the incident, stating that it does not tolerate antisemitism or any other form of racism. The university's Jewish society has also spoken out against the incident, with the president saying that Nazi glorification and antisemitism have no place in universities.\n\n**Oxford University Conservative Association Incident**\n\nIn a separate incident, the Daily Telegraph reported claims that members of the Oxford University Conservative Association regularly sang an antisemitic song with Nazi references. This incident highlights a broader issue of antisemitism and racism on university campuses.\n\n**Reactions and Statements**\n\nThe LSE's student newspaper, the Beaver, reported that the game played was a Nazi-themed version of the drinking game Ring of Fire. The Jewish student who suffered the injury told the Guardian that the comments made during the game were \"extremely upsetting\" and an \"affront to my identity.\"\n\nThe students' union and the LSE athletics union have condemned the incident, with the general secretary of the students' union saying that it is \"so at odds with everything the LSE and the union stand for.\" The president of the LSE athletics union also condemned the actions of \"a small group of individuals,\" stating that being in the athletics union is about being a team and upholding respectful behavior.\n\nOverall, these incidents highlight the need for universities to take a strong stance against antisemitism and racism, and to provide a safe and inclusive environment for all students.",
  "answers": [
    "A Nazi drinking game played by students from the London School of Economics sparked a brawl and ended with a broken nose, reports the Guardian. The row occurred during a ski trip taken by 150 members of the school's athletic union to Val D'Isere in France. The confrontation occurred after a Jewish student objected to the drinking game, according to the school's Jewish Society. The game involved playing cards arranged on a table in the shape of a swastika. Players were required to \"salute the führer,\" according to the student newspaper. \"Nazi glorification and anti-semitism have no place in our universities,\" said the head of the Jewish Society. A video of the drinking game was briefly posted on Facebook. The school is investigating. \"These are disturbing allegations relating to events which took place on a foreign trip organized by the students' union,\" said a school statement, which added that officials are \"prepared to take disciplinary action. Students must abide by clear standards of behavior. We do not tolerate anti-semitism or any other form of racism.\""
